CARD PICTURES (public/cards, 78 files)
  What: the Pixie-Smith tarot deck (Pamela Colman Smith's 1909 drawings; "Pixie" was her nickname), scanned from Wikipedia and cleaned up slightly, resized to
  300 x 527 px with a small black border.
  Original art: Pamela Colman Smith, for the deck published in 1909. The cleaner of the scans judges the
  card illustrations to be in the public domain (under Australian law) and asks users to check their own
  country.
  Scans and clean-up: Luciella Elisabeth Scarlett (luciellaes),
  https://luciellaes.itch.io/rider-waite-smith-tarot-cards-cc0 . Her page releases the card-back design under
  CC0 1.0 (https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/); credit is optional and is given here with
  thanks. She asks that the cards are not sold as they are, and that is respected: they are used inside an app
  with its own work added.
  Found through: tarotjs by berkough, https://berkough.itch.io/tarotjs (the tarotjs code is under the BSD
  3-clause licence; none of that code is used in this app, and its licence is not what permits the pictures).
  Public-domain research (2026-10-18, web sources, not legal advice): the 1909 art is public domain in the
  United States (published before 1929; US copyright is federal, so a state such as Illinois adds nothing) and
  in countries with a life-plus-70 term, because Smith died in 1951 (public domain there from 1 January 2022).
  Israel's term is life plus 70 under its 2007 law (it was life plus 50 before), so the art is out of copyright
  there either way. The later US Games Systems edition (1971) is not covered by this: only its new material is
  protected, and the pictures here are the 1909 art. The owner believes the pictures are public domain in
  their country (IL) and the research agrees; this is the owner's judgement to keep on record.

